 ],
 "literal": "Without saying 'We will know (and do it) then', do virtue; indeed, that, at the time of perishing, is an imperishable companion.",
 "translation": "Do not delay, saying, 'We will practice virtue later.' Do it now, for that virtue will be an imperishable companion when you perish.",
 "modern": "‘பிறகு பார்த்துக் கொள்ளலாம்’ என்று தள்ளிப்போடாமல் அறத்தைச் செய்ய வேண்டும்; அதுவே நாம் அழியும் காலத்தில் அழியாத துணையாக இருக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet emphasizes the urgency of practicing virtue without procrastination. The phrase 'அன்று அறிவாம்' represents the attitude of delaying good deeds to old age or a future time. The author advises doing virtue immediately because when the body perishes ('பொன்றுங்கால்'), only the virtue performed remains as an undying companion ('பொன்றாத் துணை') to support the soul. The contrast between the perishing body and the imperishable companion highlights the eternal value of righteous actions.",
